Make find -empty on a directory it cannot open set exit status 1.
-empty reported a directory it could not open and treated it as not
empty, but left the exit status alone. The search usually failed on the
same directory anyway, but not when it was not entered, as with
"-maxdepth 0" or after -prune, and then find exited 0 after printing an
error. The failure now sets the exit status, as in GNU find.

---
 findutils/find.c |    1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

--- a/findutils/find.c
+++ b/findutils/find.c
@@ -980,6 +980,7 @@
 		dir = opendir(fileName);
 		if (!dir) {
 			bb_simple_perror_msg(fileName);
+			G.exitstatus = EXIT_FAILURE;
 			return FALSE;
 		}
 
